	• Mistakes are corrected only via a reversal—a new offsetting transaction linked via reverses_transaction_id (each transaction can only be reversed once).
	• Account flipping on reversals: A REVERSAL swaps source_acc_id and target_acc_id from the original transaction so every ledger row is self-contained (e.g., reversing an expense with source = A creates a reversal with target = A, flowing money back into A).
• Strict Transaction Field Invariants (Enforced in Transaction Model):
	• INCOME: source = null, target = required, reverses_transaction_id = null, amount > 0.
	• EXPENSE: source = required, target = null, reverses_transaction_id = null, amount > 0.
	• TRANSFER: source = required, target = required, source != target, reverses_transaction_id = null, amount > 0.
	• REVERSAL: Flips original source/target (source != target, at least one set), reverses_transaction_id = required, amount > 0.
• ID Generation & No Duplicate State:
	• Store "accounts" as a JSON list ([{"id": "acc_...", ...}]) rather than repeating the ID in both a dict key and inner field.
	• Generate prefixed random IDs (acc_..., trx_...) and check for collisions before inserting so existing records can never be overwritten.

2. Unified "Never Negative" Rule (BR-10)

• One Single Implementation:
	• Defined in one shared function in services/ (validate_account_timeline(account_id, candidate_transactions)).
	• Sorts all transactions touching account_id by (transaction_date, insertion_order) and verifies running_balance >= 0 at every single point in time.
	• Every write operation (INCOME, EXPENSE, TRANSFER, and REVERSAL) runs through this exact same timeline check—no separate balance - amount >= 0 formula.
• Backdated Transactions:
	• Allowed (including dates before account.created_at), provided the chronological running balance never dips below ₦0 on any date.
	• Transactions sharing the same transaction_date are ordered deterministically by insertion order (append position in .storage.json).

3. Timezones & Validation Boundaries

• Explicit Timezone Rule:
	• System audit timestamps (account.created_at): Always timezone-aware UTC (datetime.now(timezone.utc)).
	• User-facing calendar dates & "today" filters: Always resolved in the user's local timezone (e.g., Africa/Lagos).
• Separate Write Validation from Read Filters:
	• Writing a transaction: Reject future dates (transaction_date > today in local timezone).
	• Searching / reporting: Allow future end_date values so full calendar presets (e.g., "Sept 1 to Sept 30" run mid-month) work cleanly. Only reject inverted ranges (start_date > end_date).

4. Account Lifecycle & Visibility

• Active Balances vs. Visibility:
	• Main balance list: Shows only active accounts by default.
	• Reactivation & inspection: Dedicated Deactivated Accounts view lets users find, inspect, and reactivate closed accounts.
	• Transaction filters: Deactivated accounts remain selectable in filters so historical reports stay complete.
• Zero-Balance Deactivation Rule:
	• On deactivation: Verify derived balance equals 0.
	• On transaction create / reversal: Verify all referenced accounts are active—block any new transactions or reversals on deactivated accounts so their balance stays 0.

5. Architecture, Startup Recovery & Persistence

• Unidirectional Dependency Rule & Naming:
	• Core Domain (models/, exceptions.py): Zero outward dependencies. Account and Transaction validate their own fields in __post_init__ / from_dict(). Custom errors use PEP 8 *Error naming (StorageWriteError).
	• Services (services/): Holds business workflows and validate_account_timeline(); imports models/ and exceptions.py.
	• Storage (storage/): Imports only models/ and exceptions.py (never services/), using from_dict() / to_dict(). Folder/module names match across spec text and diagrams.
• Persistence & Scaling Trigger (v1):
	• JSON storage chosen for human readability, zero schema migrations during v1, and direct mapping to to_dict() / from_dict().
	• Capped at 5,000 transactions (~1–2 MB); crossing 5,000 transactions, slow saves, or multi-user needs triggers migrating storage/ to SQLite.
• Atomic Writes & Strict Startup Recovery:
	• Saves write to .storage.json.tmp, call flush() + os.fsync(), and swap via os.replace().
	• Startup case 1 (No file): Initialize empty state cleanly.
	• Startup case 2 (Valid file): Delete any stale .storage.json.tmp (log warning), load via from_dict(), and run validate_account_timeline() on all accounts.
	• Startup case 3 (Corrupted/unreadable file or timeline violation): Fail fast and halt startup with an error—never fall back to an empty state that could overwrite the real file on the next save.
