Here is your complete, updated Master Design Decisions & Architecture Log, structured around What, Why, How, and Limitations / Trade-offs for every decision we finalized.

Master Design Decisions & Architecture Log


1. Money Representation (Integer Minor Units)

• What: Store, calculate, and serialize all monetary amounts as whole integers in the currency's smallest subdivision (e.g., 45000 kobo = ₦450.00), paired with an explicit currency code.
• Why: Binary floating-point numbers cannot represent decimal fractions accurately (0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3), causing cumulative drift in financial totals.
• How: Transaction.from_dict() enforces Python int values (rejecting floats/booleans), and formatting to major display units happens only in ui/.
• Limitations: Fractional calculations (such as percentage splits, interest, or FX conversions) still require explicit decimal math and defined rounding rules.

2. Single Source of Truth for Balances

• What: Account balances are derived exclusively from the transaction ledger (credits - debits). No balance field is stored or cached on Account.
• Why: Storing both a cached balance and a transaction history creates two sources of truth that can drift out of sync.
• How: services/ sums target_acc_id inflows minus source_acc_id outflows on demand.
• Limitations: Balance lookup is O(n) over an account's transactions; fast at personal scale (~5,000 transactions), but would require caching or SQL indexing at high volume.

3. Append-Only Ledger & Self-Contained Reversals

• What: Transactions are immutable (never edited or deleted). Mistakes are undone only by recording a new REVERSAL transaction linked via reverses_transaction_id (at most once per original transaction).
• Why: Preserves a complete, tamper-evident financial audit trail while still letting users fix errors.
• How: A REVERSAL flips source_acc_id and target_acc_id from the original transaction so money flows in the exact opposite direction (making every row self-contained for balance math).
• Limitations: Cannot correct typos in descriptions or categories in place—users must reverse and re-enter the transaction.

4. Unified Chronological Balance Rule (BR-10) & Backdating

• What: No account's running balance may drop below ₦0 on any date in history. Backdated transactions (including dates before account.created_at) are allowed as long as this rule holds.
• Why: Prevents modeling spending money before the account actually held funds on that date, while allowing users to log past receipts out of order.
• How: One shared function in services/—validate_account_timeline(account_id, transactions)—sorts transactions by (transaction_date, insertion_order) and verifies running_balance >= 0 at every step. Every write (including reversals) and startup's _validate_chronological_balances() calls this single function.
• Limitations: Strict chronological checking rejects a backdated expense if the funding income was dated later, even if today's total balance is positive.

5. Timezones & Read vs. Write Validation Boundaries

• What: System timestamps (account.created_at) use UTC; user-facing dates and "today" filters use the user's local timezone (e.g., Africa/Lagos). Writes block future dates; read filters allow future end_date values.
• Why: Eliminates ambiguity around midnight (when UTC and UTC+1 disagree on the date) and allows full calendar-month search presets (e.g., "Sept 1 to Sept 30" run on Sept 15) to work without failing.
• How: datetime.now(timezone.utc) sets created_at; local timezone .date() validates transaction_date <= today on writes, while search queries only check start_date <= end_date.
• Limitations: Assumes single-timezone local usage; traveling across timezones could shift what "today" resolves to locally.

6. Account Lifecycle, Visibility & Zero-Balance Invariant

• What: Deactivated accounts are excluded from the main balance list by default, remain visible in a dedicated Deactivated Accounts view (for inspection/reactivation) and in transaction history filters, and must always hold a ₦0 balance.
• Why: Keeps daily balance views clean without hiding historical spending or stranding closed accounts so users can't find them to reactivate.
• How: Deactivation checks derived_balance == 0; all transaction and reversal creations verify that referenced accounts are active, blocking any mutations on deactivated accounts.
• Limitations: Deactivated accounts cannot receive refunds or reversals until explicitly reactivated first.

7. Layer Architecture, Domain Invariants & Omitted Storage Protocol

• What: Unidirectional folder architecture: ui/ → services/ → file_manager/, with models/ and exceptions.py (StorageReadError, StorageWriteError) at the dependency-free base. Storage Protocol is deliberately omitted in v1.
• Why: Prevents circular imports, keeps domain entities (Account, Transaction) self-validating via __post_init__ / from_dict(), and avoids interface boilerplate for a single local JSON backend.
• How: file_manager/ imports only models/ and exceptions.py (never services/). Both layers pass WalletEngineData (TypedDict defined in models/). Tests use tmp_path or mocked I/O.
• Limitations: Migrating from JSON to SQLite later will require updating imports in services/ (or introducing a Protocol at that point).

8. Copy-on-Write State, Atomic Persistence & Startup Recovery

• What: Whole-file JSON persistence (indent=2, accounts stored as a list to avoid duplicate IDs) designed for an advisory target of up to ~5,000 transactions, paired with atomic writes and fail-fast startup recovery.
• Why: Keeps v1 data human-readable with zero schema migrations, while guaranteeing that failed writes never corrupt live memory or disk state, and failed loads never overwrite real user files with an empty state.
• How:
	• Writes: services/ copies WalletEngineData containers (dict(accounts), list(transactions)) and replaces modified Account objects with new instances (never mutating in place). file_manager/ writes .storage.json.tmp, calls flush() + os.fsync(), and swaps via os.replace().
	• Startup: load_app_data() deletes any stale .tmp file (logging a warning), returns empty state if .storage.json is missing, or raises StorageReadError on malformed JSON, duplicate IDs, broken references (_validate_loaded_data()), or negative timeline balances (_validate_chronological_balances()), halting startup immediately.
• Limitations: Full-file rewrites (O(n) per save) do not support multi-process concurrency, database isolation, or scales well beyond ~5,000 transactions (which serves as the trigger to migrate file_manager/ to SQLite).
